Well, I did it again. I beat this infuriatingly difficult yet
strangely addictive game known as Nethack. This was my fifth
ascension, the other four being a valkyrai, a samurai, a rouge, and
another valkyrai (this one atheist). I was getting tired of playing
warrior-type characters, so decided for my next game I'd try playing a
wizard-type character: an elvish wizard. Since this was my first
wizard for a while (not to mention I really suck at spell casters), I
decided to ignore conducts this time. This is quite long so be
warned...

Obvious spoiler warning here.

The wizard was name ‘Merdalf' (no surprises where this name came
from!). The initial inventory contain no particularly good potions,
identify and enchant armour scrolls, a spell book of charm monster
(this saved me many times) and a ring of regeneration (also very
useful for surviving early levels). Merdalf also had a very high
intelligence, 19, but was weak, even for a wizard (7 strength).
Merdalf had good luck in the beginning levels of the dungeon, finding
three altars before the sokoban, and three shops. One of the shops was
a scrolls shop, which provided enchant armour, enchant weapon, and
five scrolls of identify (these were blessed). The oracle statues
contained a spell book of sleep and healing, both very good in the
early game, and to train the skills for more powerful spells. Merdalf
completed the first two level of the sokoban for the food, then headed
for the gnomish mines.

Merdalf entered the gnomish mines with three pets: a giant mimic named
‘ditto' (not very useful), a pet winter wolf named ‘Frosty' (very
useful) and a pet gold golem named ‘Richy Rich'. He descended to gnome
town with little trouble, casting sleep everywhere and using a magic
whistle to summon help. The altar in the town was co-aligned, so
Merdalf sacrificed like mad to gain Magicbane. Magicbane was enchanted
to +2 when gained, so it was just left the way it was. The town itself
contain heaps of candles (25 or so) and a magic lamp. The magic lamp
was blessed to gain red dragon scale mail (I wanted silver, but made a
typo!). Sadly Frosty was killed by Izchak, and the other two pets were
left behind. Without help Merdalf barely escaped from danger several
times, but eventually descended further. The bottom of the gnomish
mines was the gnome kings cellar, and Merdalf gain the spell of
fireball, along with the luckstone and lots of booze. At mine town
this was converted to holy water, but not before angering the guards
(who were blow apart with force bolt). Merdalf was level 10 by now,
the best I'd ever got a wizard, so I got carried away and killed all
the shopkeepers (except Izchak, who fell down a trap door and was
never seen again!) I had encountered using fireball. The gold was then
donated to the priest to gain four points of protection.

Merdalf next finished the sokoban to gain an amulet of reflection, and
continued downwards. The dungeon contain amazingly few special rooms,
only one military barracks was generated, along with a shop in dungeon
level 18! Merdalf set up camp on dungeon level 17 (there was an altar
there). He cast create monster, sacrificed, prayed for a boon,
identified some loot at the shop, cast create monster etc. until he
was crowned, was expert in casting create monster, and had a lot of
loot! Merdalf's AC was still only -15, so he went back to donate to
the priest in mine town, and tried (unsuccessfully) to polypile a
magic marker. After this he went downwards, and explored down to
Medusa's level, and returned to his camp. Unfortunately, he was still
only level 12, so couldn't do the quest. Merdalf wandered up and down
the dungeon a few times, getting to level 13, and gained the magic
mapping spell when praying. Merdalf proceeded to stand on his altar,
and cast light over and over again until he had #enhanced the skill
enough to cast magic mapping at only 12% fail rate. Soon the main
dungeon was magic mapped, and the portal to Fort Ludios had been
found.

Merdalf entered Fort Ludios, and began attacking monster with
Magicbane (still his only artefact). When the soldier came in he cast
light, then fireball, killing most of the soldiers. The rest of them
were finished off with Magicbane. This technique was repeated in the
main structure. Merdalf finally reached experience level 14. The loot
was typical of Ludios: piles of gold, large quantities of useless
armour, and more food than one can carry. However he did also gain a
wand of death. He donated this to his priest for some more protection,
then headed off to the quest.

The quest was surprisingly easy at first, and Merdalf simply ignored
his magical abilities until he reached the final level. I found it
quite amusing seeing prisoners and a rouge locked up! (This was the
first time I'd been on the quest remember). The dark one was a pain to
kill, as he resisted everyone of my spells, even finger of death. He
was chased to the staircase, and began summoning like mad. Merdalf
cast charm monster over and over, ending up with a room almost full of
pets. The dark one escaped up stairs, but he was followed and jabbed
to death with Magicbane. After returning to his leader, Merdalf
relearned a bunch of spells, took his best spell books, and went to
the Medusa level.

Medusa provided absolutely not threat whatsoever, however there was a
titan on the level which caused some trouble. Thankfully Magicbane
came through and absorbed all the curses. Another problem was the
gremlins. By the time Merdalf was through with the titan, and summoned
creatures, there were about 50 of them blocking the way. I got really
tired of the ‘Do you really want to attack the gremlin?' message, and
just blasted them with cone of cold, also creating an ice bridge at
the same time. There were a few level in between Medusa and the
castle, one normal and one maze. Merdalf reached the castle, and read
his blessed scroll of earth I'd saved since the sokoban to destroy the
water near the entrance. The bridge was blow to pieces with a wand of
striking, and the various monsters were shredded with assorted spells.
Once Merdalf reached the inner castle, he killed the soldiers easily
as they were still in their barracks. The throne provided a wish:
three blessed scrolls of charging, but there was no amazing loot from
the monsters. Merdalf jumped over the trapdoors and killed the
dragons, then searching the loot they guarded. Not one useful item at
all. Merdalf jumped back again, and retrieved the wand of wishing,
filling out his ascension kit: helm of brilliance, speed boots, cloak
of magic resistance, t-shirt, gauntlets of dexterity and his red
dragon scale mail for a grand total of -39AC. The rest of the wishes
were spent on magic markers. Lichs, mind flayers and water creatures
were genocided from one magic marker, the other two providing 11
scrolls of charging. These were used to increase Merdalf's total power
from about 200 to 320. Merdalf then jumped down to the valley of the
dead. The valley of the dead was insanely easy, and four spell books
were gain from the chests and boxes, along with some potions of gain
level, wraith corpses and potions of full healing. The priestess of
Moloch was killed, and Merdalf entered the mazes of menace.

The mazes of menace were, as usual, boring. The magic mapping spell
quickened the process, and Merdalf reached the demon lords in no time.
Juiblex was already killed, two demons were bribed off (one for 4 gold
and one for 1 gold), and Orcus was stoned (with a fully charged wand
of death). However, about this point something strange happened. After
a fight with several disenchanters (damn cursed scrolls of genocide),
I checked Merdalf's AC, and it was reduced by six. I checked his
inventory, and the speed boots had disappeared! This confused me, as I
had not taken them off since the castle, and I definitely had them
when fighting with Orcus. Merdalf looked around, and found the boots
on the Orcus level, near his former statue. I still have no idea how
this happened. Anyway, Merdalf mapped and cut through some more of the
mazes, to find that he had forgotten the spell of light. This was
annoying as many of Merdalf's spells (fireball, jump etc) required
light, and I had not taken it, regarding it as a non-important spell
at the time. Merdalf invoked the eye to return to main dungeon, and
re-read the spell book, taking it with him this time. After
sacrificing a bit while he was waiting to use the eye again, he prayed
and got a spell book of identify. This annoyed me as I had identified
everything the hard way earlier. Merdalf used the eye to return to
Vlad's tower, and finished off Vlad. After some more mapping and
digging, Merdalf suddenly noticed a horrible thing: his amulet of
reflection had disappeared! Again, I had not taken it off since I
first gain it in the sokoban, and had not other source of reflection.
Merdalf searched several levels for it, and gave up. As he continued
down, he happened to come across not one, but TWO black dragons!
Merdalf only just managed to avoid being disintegrated (no
resistance), and had the beams miss him three times before they were
dead. Needless to say he ate one corpse. Merdalf continued, and soon
reached the bottom of the dungeon.  After digging up half the level to
find the vibrating square, he luckily found another amulet of
reflection and put it on. Then he went off to visit the wizard...

The Wizard of Yendor himself was no problem whatsoever, and died to
the finger before getting a single turn. The other monster did,
however, present a problem. In particular note, one umber hulk
confused Merdalf, and before I realised it he had fallen into the
water and blank a few of his best spell books, two of which he had no
copies of at his stash (sleep and confuse monster). Merdalf began to
sprint down to the vibrating square, having no problems from the
wizard at all ("you feel vaguely nervous"). Merdalf performed the
invocation, and ploughed through the fields of undead and priests of
moloch using cone of cold. (with the help of a purple worm named
‘Satation') The high priest got the finger, and Merdalf grabbed the
amulet, naming it "THE REAL ONE yay! :-)". He began to sprint upwards.
Little trouble from Rodney, LOTS of trouble from ‘a mysterious force'.
This was also the first time I found out spell costs increased while
carrying the amulet (Why doesn't this effect self-teleports used from
teleportitis?). Teleportitis helped a lot. Incidentally, on the way up
a found the amulet of reflection near my camp, along with three
artefacts being generated (Sunsword, Mjolnir and Vorpal blade) After a
while Merdalf was out of the dungeons (after refreshing some spells,
and carrying as many spell books as he could)...

Plane of earth: Killed Rodney again, and learned the dig spell. I was
unfortunately a 61% fail rate. I then realised that despite having
brought a few scrolls of gold detection (for earth and air), I had no
way to confuse myself until one of Merdalf's spells timed out. Merdalf
practised casting dig while he was waiting, improving it to only 20%
fail. Wait for ages, killed monsters, tamed four purple worms (named
‘Bloatation', ‘fatty', ‘purple dawn' and ‘Purple beast') simply
because I loved hearing ‘Burrrrp!'. After 2000 or so turns, I gave up,
and proceeded to dig out the whole area. Needless to say this took
some time (I literally dug out about 7/8 of the level), but the portal
was found (and digging was at 0% fail).

Plane of air: Not as much trouble as the plane of earth but only
because I got lucky and a monster ran into the portal almost as soon
as I entered. The air elementals hurt a lot, but Merdalf was casting
charm monster everywhere. It was a bit difficult however, as Merdalf
had no form of levitation. Lost the amulet at one point, and killed
about six monster before I found the one holding it.

Plane of fire: I originally thought this would be a lot easier, the
portal was located very early. However things turned VERY nasty when
two Archons managed to get close, and Merdalf was subject to frequent
power attacks. He'd taken out his potions of full healing, and had
them destroyed by a fire trap the next turn. The Archons began
summoning like mad, and Merdalf had no choice but to cast charm
monster over and over again. Things looked very bad for him when his
HP got down to only 50, but Archons were charmed, and the other
monster taken out with the pets he had made. Pw was also dangerously
low, 100 out of 380. Merdalf began to move towards the portal, but saw
another two Archons coming. Thankfully he managed to get a cockatrice
corpse on the way. Merdalf managed to reach the magic portal before
the Archons anyway, so it was not needed.

Plane of water: The plane of water was easy, and a time to relax and
recuperate after the last three battles. The water elementals were
easy, and killed with no magic. The area was filled with snakes as
well, water creatures being genocided. The portal was located quickly,
and Merdalf entered the final test...

The astral plane: First turn, cast detect monsters. Pestilence to the
left, Death to the right, famine in the middle. Merdalf decided to
take on famine, simply because Famine is (IMHO) far too easy. Dropped
the amulet of Yendor and kicked open the door. Merdalf casted light,
the blasted with cone of cold until most of the enemies were gone. He
picked up the amulet and continued onwards. Grabbed a potion of Full
healing off the floor and blessed it. Merdalf zapped wands of death
everywhere, and reached the first high altar, and it was the correct
one. Merdalf blasted finger of death at famine and killed him one last
time. He paused and reflected at his adventures, grabbed two bits was
an ale at the demi-god bar, and offered the amulet of Yendor...

You offer the Amulet of Yendor to Anhur... An invisible choir sings
and you are bathed in radience... The voice of Anhur thunders:
"Congratulations, mortal!" "In return for thy service, I grant thee
the gift of Immortality!" You ascend to the status of Demigod...

You may all applaud now.

I don't know how to make a dump, so this is from memory. Merdalf had
the eye, an amulet of reflection, cloak of magic resistance, red
dragon scale mail, t-shirt, speed boots, jumping boots (not worn),
water walking boots (not worn), a helm of brilliance and gauntlets of
dexterity. He was wielding the +2 Magicbane (his only melee weapon
most of the game). The armour was all highly enchanted and
foo-proofed. He had a scroll of scare monster, a potion of full
healing, a bunch of attack wands (including three of death), a unicorn
horn, stethoscope, blindfold etc. His bag of holding contained 11 K
rations, a bunch of scrolls of little use, 10 spellbooks of spells he
couldn't afford to forget (charm monster and such), 10 potions of holy
water, and a back-up unicorn horn. Merdalf had all the usual
attributes, and managed to keep two conducts: He never changed form,
and he never polymorphed an object (I don't count not wishing for
artefacts as a proper conduct).

Goodbye Merdalf the Demigod...

